A||||8||sluppy123||Problems with my Nitrous!!||||5||
Z||000000||sluppy123||12-08-2000||09:39 PM||||I'm having problems with the N20 I've got on my GT. Every time I use the Nitrous I get a code 33 (MAP sensor), The vac lines are blown off the sensor. The check engine light turns on just after the Nitrous shuts Off. I clamped all the lines together and it split the rubber Tee, so I'm replacing all the lines with new. <P>I'm wondering if I've got the lines hooked up wrong or if the rubber lines were just old and weak and by replacing them with new the problem will be cured.<P>Anybody have any thoughts or have had this problem? Can someone explain how they have their Vac lines hooked up?<P>Thamks<P>------------------<BR>-Brian S. <BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle||||reg||5
Z||000001||hugh||12-08-2000||10:27 PM||||I've never used nitrous,but from what you have described it sounds as if you are overpressurizing your intake manifold,with your nitrous.Is it a new installation?Does it have the regulator?Do you have a pressure gauge on it?The pressure in a nitrous bottle is quite high.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shaun41178||12-09-2000||06:22 PM||||It is not overpressurizing your intake.  it seems to me that the lines were bad or something.  I have never had a problem with the lines blowing off of my map sensor and I didn't even use tie downs or clamps to keep it all togethor.  Sounds like they are weak or brittle or something.  <P>I would double check to make sure that you do have all the vacuum lines routes correctly.  if a line is not going to the proper source this could also be the cause of your problems.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||12-09-2000||07:37 PM||||Seems that vac. lines should not ever have positive pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||12-09-2000||08:36 PM||||The only thing that would blow lines off is positive pressure.In a naturally aspirated engine you will not see positive pressure.Even with nitrous you will not see pressure anywhere near high enough to blow off lines unless something is wrong with your regulator.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sluppy123||12-10-2000||03:06 PM||||Here is how I have the vacuum lines hooked up.  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=1843773&p=35166929&Sequence=0"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>-Brian S. <BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle||||reg||1
Z||000006||HYPTNOTiSE||12-10-2000||05:29 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NOSdiagram.jpg"> <P>Thats what mine's like bro.  Hope that helps.<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Got2manyfieros||12-11-2000||12:51 AM||||If you hit the nitrous at low RPM you can get a nitrous backfire and have stuff like that happen.  Also, cars running a wet system, have been known to have fuel puddle in their intake, and cause a backfire resulting in a lot of positive pressure, though I have never heard of this happening specifically to a fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||12-11-2000||10:29 AM||||i'd run my nitrous just off idle and never had a problem with it.. anything above 1500 and it'd just roast the tires.(from a stop) i used NEW vacuum lines and nylon zip tied them to their home. and never had a blow off problem.||||reg||1
